────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  COMPLEXITY TIER (issue #159)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Iteration 1 declared tier 1: single mechanism, single knob, treatment vs control
  Justification: Iter 1, single mechanism (P/D disaggregation), single treatment knob (pd-decider), treatment vs control across rate conditions.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SUMMARY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  We are testing whether BLIS faithfully reproduces the paper's central prediction about prefill/decode disaggregation: that 'Always Disaggregate' (AD) yields lower inter-token latency (ITL) but higher time-to-first-token (TTFT) compared to 'Always Local' (AL), with the ITL advantage growing as arrival rate increases. This is the first iteration validating the P/D latency model inside the simulator across a sweep of load conditions.

  * h-main runs 48 simulations: 8 arrival rates × 2 modes (AL via pd-decider=never, AD via pd-decider=always) × 3 seeds, measuring mean ITL and TTFT. Confirmation requires AD ITL < AL ITL at every rate, AD TTFT > AL TTFT at every rate, and the ITL gap increasing monotonically with rate. A queueing-theory curve fit ITL = a/(1−b·λ)+c must achieve R² > 0.95 for both modes, with b_AL > b_AD.
  * h-control-negative runs 6 simulations at rate=5 req/s (near-zero load) to verify the ITL gap between AL and AD is < 0.5 ms, confirming the disaggregation benefit is load-driven (batch interference under queueing) rather than an inherent architectural constant. Failure would suggest a fixed overhead in AL mode unrelated to utilization.
  * Each run uses 4 GPU instances (2 prefill + 2 decode for AD; 4 unified for AL), 300 requests with fixed 512 prompt / 128 output tokens, qwen3-14b model, least-loaded routing. Wall time is ~3-5 seconds per run. Key unlocked parameters (max-num-scheduled-tokens=2048, pd-transfer-bandwidth=100Gbps, latency-model=trained-physics) are documented and justified as non-confounding for the ITL comparison.
  * Diagnostics are pre-specified: if AD ITL exceeds AL ITL at any rate, investigate KV transfer contention and decode-side reservation failures; if the queueing fit is poor, check chunked-prefill effects and token budget constraints causing nonlinearity beyond the 1/(1−ρ) model.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

