============================================================
  HUMAN FINDINGS GATE
============================================================

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SUMMARY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  The main hypothesis is CONFIRMED: across all 8 arrival rates (5–130 req/s) and all 3 seeds, disaggregated (AD) inference consistently achieves lower mean inter-token latency (ITL) than aggregated (AL), while AL consistently achieves lower time-to-first-token (TTFT). The ITL gap grows monotonically with load as predicted, and a queueing-theory curve fits AD well (R²=0.956), though AL's fit is slightly below threshold (R²=0.927). The negative control confirms the effect is load-driven, not structural.

  * ITL ordering AD < AL confirmed at all 8 rates with 0 violations across 48 runs. ITL gap ranges from 0.058 ms at 5 req/s to 1.682 ms at 130 req/s, monotonically increasing as predicted.
  * TTFT ordering AD > AL also confirmed at all 8 rates — disaggregation trades higher first-token latency for better streaming decode performance. TTFT gap narrows from 9.61 ms (rate=5) to 4.21 ms (rate=130).
  * Queueing model fit (ITL = a/(1−bλ)+c): AD achieves R²=0.956 (passes 0.95 threshold); AL achieves R²=0.927 (slightly below). The b-ratio AL/AD=1.21 validates the mechanism that aggregated mode carries higher effective work per request due to mixed prefill+decode batches.
  * Negative control confirmed: at near-zero load (5 req/s), the ITL gap is just 0.058 ms (3.4% of the gap at 130 req/s), demonstrating the disaggregation advantage is almost entirely load-dependent batch interference, not a baseline architectural difference.
  * All runs completed 300/300 requests with 0 drops, confirming experiment validity. The only deviation from predictions is the AL curve-fit R² being 0.023 below threshold — a minor quantitative shortfall that does not affect directional conclusions.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

